## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design joint monocular depth and motion estimation under dynamic scenes and adverse weather, optimized for edge devices and quick adaptation to new conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular depth and scene-flow fusion in cluttered traffic
- Temporal fusion across frames for stability
- On-device 5-shot adaptation with lightweight adapters

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-architecture sketch
function forward(x) {
  const z = encoder(x); // shared features
  const d = depthHead(z);
  const s = flowHead(z);
  const o = objMotionHead(z);
  const t = temporalFuse([d, s, o]);
  return {depth: d, sceneFlow: s, objectMotion: o, fused: t};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure edge-device latency vs. accuracy across weather scenarios?
- Which datasets and metrics would you prioritize for evaluation and validation?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates real-time monocular computer vision for avatar-driven video conferencing, with emphasis on three critical constraints: sub-30ms latency for natural interaction, on-device privacy for user trust, and rapid on-device adaptation for personalized gesture vocabularies. Success requires co-designing efficient models, privacy-preserving pipelines, and lightweight adaptation mechanisms—skills essential for building consumer-grade AR/VR experiences at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular landmark detection, gesture recognition, and avatar rig mapping
- Edge latency budgets and resource-aware model architectures
- Few-shot on-device adaptation using synthetic and real data
- Privacy-by-design constraints and rigorous evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton of the avatar estimator outline
class AvatarEstimator {
  constructor(model) {
    this.model = model;
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  infer(frame) {
    const landmarks = this.model.detect(frame);
    const avatarParams = this.mapToAvatar(landmarks);
    return avatarParams;
  }

  mapToAvatar(landmarks) {
    // Map 2D landmarks to 3D avatar rig parameters
    return {
      blendShapes: this.computeBlendShapes(landmarks),
      handPose: this.estimateHandPose(landmarks)
    };
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses capability to design edge-friendly 3D pose systems for dynamic outdoor scenes, handling wind-induced blur, occlusion, and rapid adaptation to new bolt geometries with minimal on-device training.

## Key Concepts

- Edge latency and memory constraints on embedded GPUs
- Monocular 3D pose estimation (6-DoF) from moving cameras
- Few-shot on-device adaptation (2-shot)
- Synthetic-to-real transfer with domain randomization
- Robustness to motion blur and lighting variation

## Code Example

```javascript
function fineTuneAdapter(model, supportImages, supportLabels) {
  // tiny adapter fine-tune on-device
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    const batch = getBatch(supportImages, supportLabels);
    const loss = model.computeLoss(batch);
    model.backward(loss);
  }
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure generalization to new turbine models or bolt types?
- What guardrails prevent false positives in flight-critical decisions?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical monocular 6-DoF pose estimation in a realistic desktop scenario, with emphasis on on-device adaptation and strict latency constraints typical of edge AR devices.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose estimation from visual cues
- CAD-model-guided PnP integration for geometric refinement
- Synthetic+real data strategy with domain randomization
- On-device few-shot adaptation on constrained edge GPUs
- Real-time tracking under partial occlusion and clutter

## Code Example
```javascript
function computePose(keypoints, model3D) {
  const rotation = estimateRotationMatrix(keypoints, model3D);
  const translation = estimateTranslationVector(keypoints, model3D);
  return { rotation, translation };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```
